VibeOS session
==============

Installing this package changes nothing on its own: no unit is enabled, the
regular GNOME session is untouched, and the vibeos extension does not run
in it. You pick the VibeOS session in one of two ways.

With a display manager (GDM)
----------------------------

Choose "VibeOS" in the session menu at the login screen. It is GNOME's
own session with GNOME Shell started in the vibeos mode
(/usr/share/gnome-shell/modes/vibeos.json), which always runs the
shell@vibeos extension. To make it the default for automatic login,
set it as the user's session:

  busctl call org.freedesktop.Accounts /org/freedesktop/Accounts/User$(id -u USER) \
      org.freedesktop.Accounts.User SetSession s vibeos

Without a display manager
-------------------------

vibeos.service starts gnome-session on tty7 as one user, logged in by
systemd (PAM service "login"), with no display manager at all. It ships with
User=user; set your own user first, then switch over:

  systemctl edit vibeos.service       # [Service] / User=<your user>
  systemctl disable gdm.service            # or phosh.service, or whatever ran
  systemctl enable vibeos.service

The extension must then be enabled for that user, as for any extension
(gnome-extensions enable shell@vibeos), or the unit given
"ExecStart=" / "ExecStart=/usr/bin/gnome-session --session=vibeos" in the
same drop-in, which runs the extension through the session mode as GDM's
"VibeOS" does.

Docking
-------

vibeos-dock switches between vibeos.service and gnome-desktop.service (the
same session, laid out as GNOME's desktop) when an external display is
plugged in or out. It is started by a udev rule that is not active by
default, because on a plug or unplug it starts a session. To use it:

  ln -s /usr/share/vibeos-session/71-vibeos-dock.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
  udevadm control --reload

Device hooks for a plug or unplug go in /etc/vibeos-dock.d/ (each is run with
"connected" or "disconnected").

Lock screen
-----------

The lock screen's PIN or pattern is the user's own password, checked by
/usr/libexec/vibeos-auth through the PAM service /etc/pam.d/vibeos (from
vibeos-daemons). A pattern is the digits 1-9 of its dots in the order
they are joined.
